tests {
  test("returns 201 Created", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });

  test("returns the SAME lead id — no second lead was created", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().id).to.equal(bru.getVar("createdLeadId"));
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /leads — replay (idempotency contract)

  Run **Create Lead** first; this re-sends its `submissionToken` and asserts the
  response carries the *same* lead id.

  This is the living documentation of the idempotency guarantee: a double-click,
  a flaky connection, or a client retry must never produce a second lead.

  ## How it works
  The client generates one token per form session. The server namespaces it by
  channel (`WEB|<uuid>`, or `SHARE|<linkId>|<uuid>` for a share link) and stores
  it on the lead under a **unique per-agency partial index**.

  Two mechanisms back the guarantee:
  1. A lookup *before* any write returns the existing lead immediately, so a
     replay never re-runs contact resolution.
  2. If two requests genuinely race, the loser hits a duplicate-key error on the
     index, re-reads, and returns the winner's lead. That is why the guarantee
     holds under concurrency and not merely for sequential retries.

  Note the payload here is otherwise identical, but it does not need to be — the
  token alone decides. A replay is a pure no-op: nothing on the existing lead is
  updated, because the request has already been fully processed once.

  ## Response `201`
  ```json
  { "id": "<the same id Create Lead returned>" }
  ```

  ## Errors
  Same as **Create Lead**.
}
